While the huge, incestuous muddle of conflicting folklore behind the
English language means that fantasy terms like elves, pixies, fairies,
sprites end even gnomes are often used more-or-less interchangeably, I
feel sure that 95% of native english-speakers would, upon viewing a
picture of a prettily-dressed miniature girl with insect wings, say
"fairy" or perhaps "pixie" before they said "elf".

Dark-elves and drow aside, in the modern, english-speaking world, I
think the public perception of an "elf" is almost universally either
(a) tolkien/ D&Desque benevolent and/or aloof pointy-eared treehugger
or (b) felt-clad, gnome-like toy-production operative employed in
Santa's sweatshop.
Of course, a hundred years ago (or even fifty years ago) a similar
imaginary survey would have yielded different results: More malevolent
or mischieveous creatures of a more pixie-like appearance might well
have prevailed.

And of course, I'm still only talking about English-speaking cultures.
For instance, I suspect that even today in the post-Jackson genre, your
average scandinavian would cling to an older perception of elves,
having a stronger link as they do with the old Nordic legends in which
"elf" and "dwarf" were more or less synonymous, and had more in common
with the Tolkienesque picture of a short, stout, bearded,
gold-grabbing, smithying dwarf. In fact, Tolkien's elves seem more akin
to the Nordic Vanir than the Nordic elves.

Other cultures, of course, have their own traditions and folklore to
draw upon, but of course the further away from the geographical source
of the word "elf" you get, (ie nothern Europe), the less meaningful the
question becomes because, obviously, other places have their own
languages and while they may have elf-like creatures in their folklore,
they won't be called 'elves' and probably won't have very much common
ground in terms of shared folk history/ morphology. For instance, why
don't we translate the japanese "tengu" into "elf"? (That was a
rhetorical question, btw).

It would be a very interesting excercise to somehow try to quantify the
influence of nethack's various literary & mythological sources upon the
game. Anyone got any idea how this might be achieved? In fact, just
listing the sources would be quite a task...

